  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "sp_radix_size",
    "file": "wolfssl/wolfcrypt/src/sp_int.c",
    "description": "The hexadecimal sizing logic disagrees with sp_tohex for negative values when zero-padding is enabled. sp_radix_size computes the magnitude nibble count, adds one for the '-' sign, and only then rounds the combined count to an even number. sp_tohex instead emits '-' separately and rounds/pads the magnitude itself to an even number of hex digits. For a negative magnitude requiring an odd number of hex digits, sp_radix_size therefore under-reports the required buffer by one byte. A caller allocating the documented size passes that undersized buffer to sp_tohex, which writes one byte past the heap allocation while emitting the remaining digits or terminating NUL.",
    "conditions": "The fuzz input selects Mod case 3 and, during GetPtr() on the temporary Montgomery-normalization bignum, enables baseConversion and selects hexadecimal radix. The temporary value is negative and its magnitude has an odd hexadecimal digit count. With WOLFSSL_SP_INT_NEGATIVE enabled and WC_DISABLE_RADIX_ZERO_PAD not defined, baseConversion calls mp_radix_size/sp_radix_size, allocates exactly the returned size, then calls mp_toradix/sp_tohex. The incorrect ordering of sign accounting and even-digit padding makes the allocation one byte too small, producing the observed one-byte heap-buffer write."
  },
